        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The key function is `_make_keys_list`. The task: reject an empty iterable of secret keys when constructing a Signer, immediately with ValueError.\n\nLet me modify `_make_keys_list` to raise ValueError if the iterable produces no keys. Smallest change:\n\n```python\ndef _make_keys_list(\n    secret_key: str | bytes | cabc.Iterable[str] | cabc.Iterable[bytes],\n) -> list[bytes]:\n    if isinstance(secret_key, (str, bytes)):\n        return [want_bytes(secret_key)]\n\n    return [want_bytes(s) for s in secret_key]  # pyright: ignore\n```\n\nChange to:\n\n```python\n    if isinstance(secret_key, (str, bytes)):\n        return [want_bytes(secret_key)]\n\n    keys = [want_bytes(s) for s in secret_key]  # pyright: ignore\n\n    if not keys:\n        raise ValueError(\"Secret keys iterable must not be empty.\")\n\n    return keys\n```\n\nThat handles empty lists, tuples and one-shot iterators (since we materialize once). Also empty string / empty bytes keys remain individual strings/bytes, accepted — yes, because `isinstance(secret_key, (str, bytes))` catches those first.\n\nLet me also check the test file to see how tests reference things, and check whether serializer uses _make_keys_list too. Let me search for `_make_keys_list` usage.",
